# DTF tier manifest: readyz-hung-dependency  (artifact-keeper#4781 / #4796, 1.11.3)
# Profile-set: filesystem + scanners.mock-hang (a Trivy stand-in on the slot's
# dtf network whose /healthz the oracle can make HANG, and which records every
# probe it receives with how long the backend waited before giving up).
#
# THE BUG (#4781)
# /readyz probes each optional dependency (OpenSearch, storage, scanner, LDAP)
# through the same short-TTL (5 s), single-flight cache /health uses, so a
# hung dependency should cost the 2 s READYZ_DEPENDENCY_TIMEOUT once per TTL
# and every other /readyz in the window should read the cached timeout.
# readyz_cached wrapped cached_check in an OUTER 2 s timeout started before
# the cache lock wait, and the probe in an INNER 2 s timeout started after it.
# Whenever a caller waited for the lock, its outer timer fired first and
# dropped cached_check before the `*guard = Some(...)` store: nothing was
# cached, and the next /readyz probed the hung dependency again and paid the
# full 2 s. The fix (#4796) takes one Instant before the lock wait, runs the
# probe under timeout_at(deadline) and the lock wait under
# timeout_at(deadline + 100 ms), so the inner timeout always fires first and
# its result is stored.
#
# THE PRIMITIVE: A DETERMINISTIC LOCK WAIT
# Without contention the two timers start microseconds apart and the race is
# lost only when they straddle a timer tick (the CI flake in #4781). The
# oracle makes the lock wait certain: a first /readyz (curl --max-time 0.5)
# takes the scanner cache lock and starts probing the hung mock; the client
# goes away at 0.5 s, hyper drops the handler, and the lock is released with
# nothing stored. A second /readyz that arrived at 0.15 s has been waiting
# for the lock and now probes with 0.35 s of its outer deadline already
# spent. This is the shape a kubelet readiness probe with a short
# timeoutSeconds produces against a hung dependency.
#   1.11.2: the second call's outer timer fires first, nothing is cached, and
#           the third call probes the hung mock again and takes ~2 s.
#   1.11.3: the second call's inner timeout fires and is stored; the third
#           call and a burst after it answer from the cache in milliseconds
#           and the mock sees no further probe until the TTL expires.
#
# There is no backend log line per probe or per timeout (with_readyz_deadline
# does not log, and the scanner is not `required`, so /readyz does not warn),
# so "probed once per TTL" is read from the mock's own probe record instead
# of `docker logs`. The mock's "held" time separates /readyz probes (given up
# at the 2 s deadline) from the backend's 60 s health monitor, which hits the
# same URL with a 5 s client timeout.
#
# The dependency is the SCANNER, not Postgres: the /readyz database and
# migrations checks are direct uncached queries, not readyz_cached, so a hung
# database does not exercise this code path.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem scanners.mock-hang"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
